Biologically, an ant’s purpose is to survive and help its colony thrive—finding food, caring for young, maintaining the nest, or reproducing. In the wider ecosystem, ants help turn and enrich soil, disperse seeds, recycle organic matter, and regulate other insects. Philosophically, though, nature gives no single creature a preset purpose; an ant’s significance emerges from the relationships it sustains.
