Characterized by their three-part body structure—head, thorax, and abdomen—ants possess six legs equipped with tiny hairs that assist in sensory functions, particularly in communication.
They live in complex social colonies that include a queen, workers, soldiers, and males, each playing specific roles such as nursing and foraging.
Ants build various types of nests, from simple dirt mounds to intricate chambers connected by tunnels, which serve as nurseries, food storage, and resting areas.
...
They play vital ecological roles, being significant predators of small invertebrates and essential seed dispersers, contributing to soil aeration.
Ants have developed various defense mechanisms, including poison sacs and stingers, to protect themselves from predators.
